WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.680
Charles Blagden went into a room heated to about 200 degrees F. A thermometer under

2
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.580
his tongue read 98 degrees, about one degree above his usual.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.260
An egg in hot air heats until it matches the air.

4
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.360
A living person did not.

5
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:25.323
Biologists call this homeostasis, the holding of a body variable within a range

6
00:00:25.323 --> 00:00:27.083
compatible with staying alive.

7
00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:30.223
The standard model of a loop has five parts.

8
00:00:30.943 --> 00:00:35.423
The regulated variable is the quantity held, and the set point is its reference

9
00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:40.523
value. The sensor measures the variable, and the integrator compares the sensed

10
00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:43.023
value with the set point and sends a command.

11
00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:47.883
The effector is a muscle, gland or other organ that changes the variable.

12
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.057
In an adult human, the variable is core temperature.

13
00:00:53.357 --> 00:00:56.597
Temperature-sensitive nerve cells in the skin and brain are the sensors.

14
00:00:57.497 --> 00:01:00.617
The hypothalamus, a region of the brain, is the integrator.

15
00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:05.777
When temperature rises, the effectors include blood vessels in the skin, which

16
00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:09.957
widen, and sweat glands, whose sweat removes heat as it evaporates.

17
00:01:10.757 --> 00:01:15.397
When it falls, the effectors include skeletal muscles, which shiver and release heat

18
00:01:15.397 --> 00:01:16.517
as they use ATP.

19
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:20.337
If temperature rises, sweating brings it down.

20
00:01:21.177 --> 00:01:22.877
If it falls, shivering brings it up.

21
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:27.977
Both are negative feedback, because each response pushes the variable back toward

22
00:01:27.977 --> 00:01:30.917
the set point. The set point itself can move.

23
00:01:31.797 --> 00:01:36.197
In a fever it rises, so a person at normal temperature feels cold and shivers.

24
00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:42.367
A regulator holds a variable fairly steady across outside conditions, and a

25
00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:44.287
conformer lets it follow the environment.

26
00:01:45.287 --> 00:01:47.527
An adult human regulates core temperature.

27
00:01:48.307 --> 00:01:53.187
A typical teleost fish in a lake is an ectotherm whose body temperature largely

28
00:01:53.187 --> 00:01:54.187
follows the water.

29
00:01:54.927 --> 00:01:57.387
That is a different solution, not a lesser one.

30
00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:03.107
For blood salt, the shore crab conforms in full-strength seawater and regulates in

31
00:02:03.107 --> 00:02:04.107
diluted water.

32
00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:06.567
Regulation is not free.

33
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:12.627
In single-housed male mice of one laboratory strain, energy expenditure was about

34
00:02:12.627 --> 00:02:16.467
30% % higher at 22 degrees C than at 30.

35
00:02:17.287 --> 00:02:22.427
A mouse is small and loses heat fast, so at 22 degrees it spends more to stay warm.

36
00:02:23.387 --> 00:02:25.867
Name the variable first, then the loop that holds it.
